---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { wrapperTree } from "../../../demos/wrapper";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

/*
 * The tree is ONE Wrapper, not four. `md`, `lg` and `full` all just fill this page's own stage
 * (`<main>` is itself `data-size="md"`, ~907px of ceiling), so comparing them there would show
 * three identical boxes: that was measured before this tree existed. `sm` is the one ceiling
 * that reads: its column ends up narrower than the stage and centred, which is exactly the one
 * thing a tree can demonstrate about a layout. The other three sizes are a fact about a number,
 * not about layout, and stay in the table below instead of the stage.
 */

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
---

<Base title="Wrapper" description="Wrapper: Page column with a ceiling width of one scale.">
  <h1>Wrapper</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Wrapper is the page column: maximum centered width, with inline padding. It is what others
    they call <em>container</em>, but that name is already from the <em>container queries</em>, so here the
    role is called <strong>wrapper</strong>. The size is a <strong>scale</strong>
    (<code>sm</code>, <code>md</code>, <code>lg</code>, <code>full</code>), not a usage name: the
    ceiling tells how wide the column can grow; the work it does is decided by whoever writes it.
  </p>

  <h2>A width ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Each size is a different <strong>ceiling width</strong>: the column centres and stops growing
    upon reaching it. Below, a Wrapper with <code>data-size="sm"</code>: the margin on either
    side is the ceiling doing its job.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={wrapperTree(t)} label="Wrapper" flush />

  <p>
    Under its ceiling, any wrapper is simply <code>100%</code>, so on a narrow screen all four
    sizes look the same, and they only separate when there is room. Ceilings are stable: density
    changes spacing and controls, never the column's maximum width.
  </p>

  <h2>The four ceilings</h2>
  <table class="sk-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Ceiling</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>sm</code></td><td>42rem</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>md</code></td><td>64rem · default</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>lg</code></td><td>90rem</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>full</code></td><td>no ceiling, the entire viewport</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>on this site</h2>
  <p>
    This documentation wraps itself, with two steps. The header and three-column shell both carry <code>data-size="lg"</code> (and the site tunes the ceiling to its own value), so a single number keeps its edges aligned. The column you are reading is <code>md</code>: the <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> carries <code>data-size="md"</code> and its ceiling is the token, not a copied number. And a full-screen tool, like the configurator, drops the wrapper altogether.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>In HTML, add <code>sk-wrapper</code> to the element delimiting the column.</li>
    <li>
      <code>data-size</code> accepts <code>sm</code>, <code>md</code> (default), <code>lg</code> or <code>full</code> (no roof).
    </li>
    <li>
      The ceilings are tier 2 tokens: <code>--size-wrapper-sm</code>, <code>--size-wrapper-md</code>, <code>--size-wrapper-lg</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The only hook that a consumer tunes is <code>--sk-wrapper-max</code>, for a width that the scale does not yet name, without reimplementing centering or padding.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
